1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,874
A diabetic patient  is coming for surgery.  What do you check?

2
00:00:05,504 --> 00:00:09,158
Half of all diabetic patients  will need surgery  in their lifetime.

3
00:00:09,558 --> 00:00:12,458
Type one  makes no insulin.  Type two  resists it.

4
00:00:12,858 --> 00:00:17,980
Fasting glucose  above one hundred twenty-six  is hyperglycemia,  risking ketoacidosis.

5
00:00:18,379 --> 00:00:22,747
Before surgery,  call the physician:  how well controlled,  and any complications?

6
00:00:23,147 --> 00:00:27,257
Screen the heart  with an EKG  The risk of silent ischemia  is higher.

7
00:00:27,657 --> 00:00:31,338
Autonomic neuropathy  links to silent heart attacks  and sudden death.

8
00:00:31,737 --> 00:00:35,618
Gastroparesis  delays stomach emptying,  raising the risk  of aspiration.

9
00:00:36,018 --> 00:00:40,394
Diet-controlled,  minor procedure?  Glucose under two hundred  needs no change.

10
00:00:40,794 --> 00:00:47,194
Major surgery  means over one hour of anesthesia,  or glucose above two hundred.  Monitor hourly,  and give insulin.

11
00:00:47,594 --> 00:00:52,737
Surgical stress  can tip stable patients  onto insulin.  Then manage them  as type one.

12
00:00:53,137 --> 00:00:57,140
Oral drugs:  first-generation sulfonylureas  stop three days before.

13
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:01,027
Second-generation  can continue  until the morning of surgery.

14
00:01:01,427 --> 00:01:05,665
Metformin and glitazones  stop the night before,  for lactic acidosis risk.

15
00:01:06,635 --> 00:01:11,787
Assess control.  Screen the heart.  Time the drugs by class.  Save this for your next surgical case.
